WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 2.1 · Theory of Change
Understanding Theory of Change

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
A Theory of Change lets you test your idea on paper before you launch.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:31.096
A Theory of Change makes your reasoning explicit: if we do this work, why might a particular change follow? It runs from the problem to inputs, activities, outputs, outcomes and long-term impact. In the next lessons we build it one step at a time.

4
00:00:31.496 --> 00:00:32.096
Three spheres, one pathway

5
00:00:32.096 --> 00:00:45.278
Control: Inputs, activities and outputs: what you put in, what you do and what you deliver. For example: Two founders’ time and a shared-kitchen slot; a weekly stall and four trainees in year one.

6
00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:55.126
Influence: Outcomes: changes in the people and institutions you work with directly. For example: Trainees gain baking skills and confidence; neighbours buy good local bread.

7
00:00:55.126 --> 00:01:06.544
Contribute: Impact: wider change that many actors shape, so your claims get more modest. For example: More young people in the neighbourhood in good, lasting work.

8
00:01:06.944 --> 00:01:07.544
Our bakery’s pathway, in order

9
00:01:07.544 --> 00:01:14.330
Problem: young people who left school early cannot get a first job without experience.

10
00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:21.115
Inputs and activities: a shared kitchen, trainee pay and a mentor; six-month paid traineeships.

11
00:01:21.115 --> 00:01:29.641
Outputs, outcomes, impact: four traineeships; skills and a first job; more young people in decent work.

12
00:01:30.041 --> 00:01:30.641
Why it matters

13
00:01:30.641 --> 00:01:35.944
1. You test your idea on paper before you spend money.

14
00:01:35.944 --> 00:01:41.989
2. It shows partners and funders why your work should lead to change.

15
00:01:41.989 --> 00:01:47.922
3. Modest claims build trust while your idea is untested.

16
00:01:48.322 --> 00:01:48.922
How you will use it

17
00:01:48.922 --> 00:01:53.115
1. One pathway for each external strategic objective.

18
00:01:53.115 --> 00:01:57.307
2. Internal objectives appear as inputs and conditions.

19
00:01:57.307 --> 00:02:02.870
3. Your indicators and reviews, later in the course.

20
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:03.870
What to avoid

21
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:09.174
A diagram of arrows with no explanation of each link.

22
00:02:09.174 --> 00:02:13.367
Claiming the wider impact as yours alone.

23
00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:20.041
Treating it as fixed instead of revising it when evidence disagrees.

24
00:02:20.641 --> 00:02:22.441
You write the change argument for your first external objective.

25
00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:26.441
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

26
00:02:27.941 --> 00:02:31.041
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
